cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[What is happening in Seattle now? Police searching for 2nd shooter; no details about arrested suspect revealed]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132662966.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A mass shooting occurred at Seattle Center's Bite of Seattle food festival. Three people died and four others sustained injuries in the chaotic incident. Police are actively searching for a second shooter involved in the event. The festival grounds were closed as vendors packed up their stands. Authorities are seeking information to identify and arrest any outstanding suspects.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/what-is-happening-in-seattle-now-police-searching-for-2nd-shooter-no-details-about-arrested-suspect-revealed/articleshow/132662966.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/what-is-happening-in-seattle-now-police-searching-for-2nd-shooter-no-details-about-arrested-suspect-revealed/articleshow/132662966.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T19:06:35+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132663090,imgsize-77022.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Six orphaned rhinos give birth after mothers were killed by poachers]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132660890.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In a heartwarming turnaround, six orphaned rhinos from South Africa have welcomed new calves after years of rehabilitation. Their successful births signify a remarkable milestone in conservation efforts, providing renewed hope for endangered rhino populations. Wildlife officials emphasized that these births not only enrich the local ecosystem but also bolster South Africa's commitment to preserving this majestic species.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/poachers-killed-their-mothers-and-left-six-young-rhinos-orphaned-after-up-to-five-years-of-rehabilitation-every-one-has-now-given-birth-in-the-wild/articleshow/132660890.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/poachers-killed-their-mothers-and-left-six-young-rhinos-orphaned-after-up-to-five-years-of-rehabilitation-every-one-has-now-given-birth-in-the-wild/articleshow/132660890.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:48:15+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132662586,imgsize-489402.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[518 million years ago, one tiny sea creature may have started the evolution of the fangs]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132660572.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/china/518-million-years-ago-one-tiny-sea-creature-may-have-started-the-evolution-of-the-fangs-seen-in-spiders-today/articleshow/132660572.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/china/518-million-years-ago-one-tiny-sea-creature-may-have-started-the-evolution-of-the-fangs-seen-in-spiders-today/articleshow/132660572.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132660572,imgsize-1356839.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Around 1200 BCE, major Mediterranean civilisations began collapsing; scientists have now reconstructed the climate conditions surrounding the crisis]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132662191.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A new study suggests prolonged drought pressured eastern Mediterranean societies.  This drying significantly affected agriculture and food security across the region.  Drought acted as a threat multiplier, worsening existing societal challenges.  Warfare and invasions interacted with environmental stress during this period.  Understanding past climate pressures informs our view of societal vulnerability.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/around-1200-bce-major-mediterranean-civilisations-began-collapsing-scientists-have-now-reconstructed-the-climate-conditions-surrounding-the-crisis/articleshow/132662191.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/around-1200-bce-major-mediterranean-civilisations-began-collapsing-scientists-have-now-reconstructed-the-climate-conditions-surrounding-the-crisis/articleshow/132662191.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:30:55+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132662381,imgsize-216844.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[One of the world's largest rodents was imported for fur farming in North America; today it's devastating wetlands and riverbanks across US]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132658929.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Nutria, large rodents introduced for fur farming, now infest forty US states and Canada.  These prolific breeders destroy vital wetland vegetation and agricultural crops like rice.  Their burrowing weakens levees and dams, posing risks to infrastructure and public safety.  Eradication efforts involve trapping, detection dogs, and bounty programs to control populations.  These invasive creatures have caused millions of dollars in damage across affected regions.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/one-of-the-worlds-largest-rodents-was-imported-for-fur-farming-in-north-america-today-its-devastating-wetlands-and-riverbanks-across-us/articleshow/132658929.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/one-of-the-worlds-largest-rodents-was-imported-for-fur-farming-in-north-america-today-its-devastating-wetlands-and-riverbanks-across-us/articleshow/132658929.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T17:18:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132659161,imgsize-198434.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[200 years after the first dinosaur, scientists use brain scans on a 100-million-year-old snake fossil from Brazil]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132661926.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Palaeontology has transformed from examining fossil fragments to digitally reconstructing ancient creatures.  A new Cretaceous snake fossil from Brazil showcases this remarkable scientific advancement.  High-resolution micro-CT scanning revealed the snake's brain anatomy and lifestyle.  This technology allows detailed comparisons across millions of years of evolution.  Modern methods are reshaping our understanding of prehistoric life's diversity.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/200-years-after-the-first-dinosaur-scientists-use-brain-scans-on-a-100-million-year-old-snake-fossil-from-brazil/articleshow/132661926.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/200-years-after-the-first-dinosaur-scientists-use-brain-scans-on-a-100-million-year-old-snake-fossil-from-brazil/articleshow/132661926.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132662023,imgsize-131967.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Lonely whooping crane that refused to mate helped save one of the world’s rarest birds]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132660771.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-1976-a-scientist-spent-seven-years-dancing-with-a-lonely-whooping-crane-that-refused-to-mate-the-chick-that-followed-helped-save-one-of-the-worlds-rarest-birds/articleshow/132660771.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-1976-a-scientist-spent-seven-years-dancing-with-a-lonely-whooping-crane-that-refused-to-mate-the-chick-that-followed-helped-save-one-of-the-worlds-rarest-birds/articleshow/132660771.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:07:19+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132660771,imgsize-1719435.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[UK's largest girls' organisation Girlguiding unveils first uniform redesign since 1990 — focused on ‘self-expression and confidence’]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132661106.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/uks-largest-girls-organisation-girlguiding-unveils-first-uniform-redesign-since-1990-focused-on-self-expression-and-confidence/articleshow/132661106.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/uk/uks-largest-girls-organisation-girlguiding-unveils-first-uniform-redesign-since-1990-focused-on-self-expression-and-confidence/articleshow/132661106.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T18:08:48+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132661931,imgsize-713613.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[The Great Pyramid was not built by slaves, but by paid workers who drank beer and ate meat.]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/132661094.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Modern archaeology says that pyramid builders were not slaves but a respected workforce.  These workers lived in a dedicated town and received good food and medical care.  Their diet included meat, bread, and beer, ensuring strength for their labor.  Burial in tombs near the pyramids honoured their significant contribution to ancient Egypt.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/the-great-pyramid-was-not-built-by-slaves-instead-it-was-built-by-paid-workers-who-drank-beer-ate-meat-received-medical-care-and-were-buried-with-honour/articleshow/132661094.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/the-great-pyramid-was-not-built-by-slaves-instead-it-was-built-by-paid-workers-who-drank-beer-ate-meat-received-medical-care-and-were-buried-with-honour/articleshow/132661094.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-07-27T17:55:26+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-132661288,imgsize-277290.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA['No other...': Parents of Indian medical student Vaibhav Duggal who died by suicide seek $30 million from Texas Tech University]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-t